  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 221 PM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nTonight through Thursday Night...\n\nAnother night of quiet and dry weather is in the forecast for \ntonight thanks to high pressure maintaining steady control over the \neastern half of the CONUS. Cloud cover will begin to increase \nslightly across portions of central MS and south MS from northwest \nto southeast later tonight with clear skies elsewhere. Sky cover \nwill be generally clear across our whole area as we head into \nmidnight. Nighttime lows will dip into the upper 40s/lower 50s. \n\nDry conditions will continue across our entire forecast area on \nThursday. Expect afternoon highs to peak into the 80s areawide. \nQuiet conditions will persist across our CWA Thursday night under \nclear skies. Overnight lows will once again dip into the upper \n40s/lower 50s. /CR/\n\nFriday through Wednesday...\n\nThe forecast messaging for the long term period remains the same \nwith no major modifications made to the overall forecast. As we head \ninto the weekend, long term guidance is beginning to show a shift to \nmore easterly low level flow starting early Friday morning. This \nshift in low level flow will result in a wedging effect around the \nAppalachians. This will cause a little dip in daytime highs across \nour CWA particularly Saturday and Sunday, with afternoon highs \npeaking in the upper 70s/lower 80s.\n\nFire weather will continue to be the primary focus over the upcoming \nweek. Antecedent ground conditions/fuels are exceptionally dry \n(daily max ERCs in the 90th+ percentile) with RHs falling to 20-30 \npercent each afternoon and at times falling into the teens. However, \nwinds will remain relatively light throughout this time frame, which \nis why we don't imminently anticipate a more critical situation \nworthy of red flag products. We went ahead and issued a fire wx \nheadline regarding increased fire danger across our forecast area for \nthis week in coordination with local forestry agencies and the U.S. \nForest Service. Furthermore, the areawide \"Limited\" threat for fire \nwx concern has been upgraded to a \"Elevated\" threat. \n\nGreat caution is still warranted with any outdoor burning over the \nnext several days, or it should be avoided altogether, as local burn \nbans continue to be added across our area. If there are any signs in \nlater forecasts of stronger wind, messaging may need to be elevated \naccordingly. Dry weather conditions will continue to persist across \nour CWA by Wednesday. /CR/DL/",
